#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f18fe80-b975-5243-8971-ac37a514c459" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 x^{2} - \\sin{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491412} {:answer/id #uuid "866b680e-b95e-59e9-a274-19e8c2a75d6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 x^{2} - \\cos{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491416} {:answer/id #uuid "a2356a72-59b5-5c9c-b674-716424d158d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 x^{2} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491419} {:answer/id #uuid "d43c8d95-d21a-5c9b-9163-9316a2bc2a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 9 x^{2} + 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491426} {:answer/id #uuid "ef497264-895e-54be-869a-0733bc78f89e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 x^{2} - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef497264-895e-54be-869a-0733bc78f89e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 18 x^{2} - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x - 12 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491433} :answer-field/id #uuid "0908c732-21de-5c29-bd3c-fcc27be47e16" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186491409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edc669f2-8811-573d-b7ea-ebf03a0592e1" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12-2x)-6{x}^{3}.$\n\n{{selection}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12-2x)$ and $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =g(x)+h(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we differentiate $g(x).$ We express $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12-2x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ng=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u,\\,u=12-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12-2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate $h(x)=-6{x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-18{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x)+{h}^{′}(x)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12-2x)-18{x}^{2}.\n$$"}]}
